## Part B — Environment Implementation

`AmericanPutEnv` implements `reset()` and `step(action)`:

- `reset(seed=None)` resets `t=0`, `S=S0`, `done=False`, returns the initial
  observation.
- `step(action)` raises `RuntimeError` if called after the episode is
  already done; otherwise it applies the transition rules from A.3 and
  returns `(obs, reward, done, info)`. Both `EXERCISE` and forced expiry
  set `done=True` — there is no path back to `HOLD` after either.

**B.4 Environment tests** (all in `american_put_env.py`, all pass):

1. **`test_nonnegative_payoff`** — runs 200 episodes under a fully random
   action sequence and asserts every single reward is `>= 0`. This checks
   the payoff formula `max(K - S, 0)` is applied correctly everywhere and
   that discounting/scaling never introduces a sign error.
2. **`test_cannot_step_after_done`** — exercises immediately, confirms
   `done=True`, then calls `step()` again and asserts it raises
   `RuntimeError` rather than silently returning a second reward. This is
   the check for "cannot exercise twice / cannot receive payoff twice."
3. **`test_single_payment_and_termination_on_expiry`** (bonus third test) —
   runs `HOLD` for the entire episode and asserts (a) the episode terminates
   at exactly `t=N` and (b) at most one nonzero reward was ever paid. This
   guards against the "reward while holding" failure mode explicitly called
   out in the assignment.

