## Part C — Policy Comparison

Parameters used for all comparisons: `S0=100, K=100, r=3%, sigma=20%,
T=1 year, N=50 steps` (i.e., roughly weekly decisions). 1000 episodes per
policy, distinct seeds per policy for independence.

| Policy                 | Avg Payoff | Avg Discounted Payoff | Std (disc.) | Exercise Rate |
|-------------------------|-----------:|-----------------------:|------------:|---------------:|
| Always-hold-to-expiry    | 6.5922     | 6.3974                 | 9.2060      | 0.00% |
| Immediate-exercise       | 0.0000     | 0.0000                 | 0.0000      | 100.00% |
| Random (5%/step)         | 3.4341     | 3.3854                 | 5.8314      | 93.20% |
| Tabular Q-learning (opt.)| 3.8868     | 3.8672                 | 3.0511      | 78.40% |

- **Immediate-exercise** always scores 0 here because `S0=K` (at‑the‑money):
  exercising at `t=0` throws away all time value for a payoff of exactly
  zero, every single episode. This is the clearest illustration in the
  whole assignment of why early exercise without a moneyness/time
  condition is a bad policy.
- **Always-hold-to-expiry** achieves the highest raw and discounted average
  payoff of the three baselines. That's expected for a put on a
  positive-drift risk-neutral process (drift `r=3%>0`): waiting lets the
  option pick up the (comparatively rare but valuable) large downside
  moves, and — since it's ATM — most of the option's expected value here
  is genuinely European-style time value rather than early-exercise
  premium.
- **Random** performs between the two, and its 93% exercise rate is
  dominated by the ~50/50 per-step choice cascading to near-certain early
  stopping over a 50-step episode, consistent with a per-step exercise
  probability compounding across many periods, most of it out-of-the-money
  and paying zero.

**Exercise-time distribution** for the two nontrivial policies:

![exercise time histograms](exercise_time_histograms.png)

`immediate_exercise` is a spike at `t=0` by construction. `random`'s
exercise times are front-loaded (highest density in the first several
steps) simply because with a constant per-step exercise probability,
survival to later steps is geometrically less likely — this is an artifact
of the policy, not of option economics.

**Optional — Part C.4: tabular Q-learning sketch.** Discretizing
`(moneyness, time-remaining)` into a 20×20 grid and running tabular
Q-learning for 20,000 episodes (`epsilon`-greedy, decaying `1.0 -> 0.05`)
gives a lower-variance, higher-average policy than random hold, with a
78% exercise rate — i.e., it learns to hold roughly a fifth of the time
all the way to expiry, and to exercise selectively otherwise. The
approximate learned exercise boundary (moneyness below which the learned
Q-function prefers `EXERCISE` over `HOLD`, per time-remaining bucket):

![learned exercise boundary](learned_exercise_boundary.png)

The boundary sits **below 1.0 moneyness** (i.e. the option must be
noticeably in-the-money, not just barely) and — despite the coarse
20-bin discretization and being far short of full convergence — is
qualitatively consistent with the American-put exercise region from
Week 4: exercise only when sufficiently in the money, with the exercise
threshold (moneyness cutoff) generally loosening as time-to-maturity
shrinks. This is a sketch, not a converged agent — Week 8 will replace it
with a properly trained policy (function approximation and/or longer
training).

