## Reflection

Of the three required baselines, **always-hold-to-expiry** behaves closest
to true American-put intuition *for this specific at‑the‑money case*,
simply because immediate exercise forfeits all time value (payoff exactly
zero) and random exercise throws away value on largely arbitrary, mostly
out‑of‑the‑money stopping times. But "always hold" is not a correct general
policy — it is only doing well here because the option starts exactly
at‑the‑money. The Week 4 boundary intuition is that early exercise is
optimal **only when the option is sufficiently deep in the money** (moneyness
comfortably below 1), because in that region the remaining time value no
longer compensates for the erosion of intrinsic value / the benefit of
receiving the strike early. Neither "always hold" nor "always exercise
immediately" encodes that moneyness- and time-dependent trade-off — only a
policy that conditions on `(moneyness, time remaining)`, like the tabular
Q-learning sketch, can. Its learned boundary (exercise once moneyness drops
below a threshold near or below 1, tightening/loosening with time
remaining) is the first baseline in this assignment that actually
approximates the shape of a real American-put exercise region, which is
exactly the behavior Week 8's stronger RL policy needs to refine and
validate at higher resolution and with better convergence.

